\b 1. Aim\b0\par
Set up Prometheus and Grafana to monitor application performance and health, and use Elasticsearch, Logstash, Kibana, and Filebeat to collect and analyze container logs.\par
\par
\b 2. Observability Concepts\b0\par
\b Metrics:\b0 Numeric measurements such as request counts, response status codes, CPU usage, memory usage, and uptime.\par
\b Prometheus:\b0 Pulls metrics from application HTTP endpoints and stores time-series data.\par
\b Grafana:\b0 Displays Prometheus data through dashboards and graphs.\par
\b Logs:\b0 Text or JSON records generated by application containers.\par
\b Filebeat:\b0 Reads Kubernetes container log files from the node.\par
\b Logstash:\b0 Receives, parses, and forwards logs.\par
\b Elasticsearch:\b0 Stores and indexes log documents for searching.\par
\b Kibana:\b0 Provides a browser interface for searching and analyzing Elasticsearch logs.\par
\par
\b 3. Monitoring Flow\b0\par
\f1 TaskFlow services -> /metrics -> Prometheus -> Grafana\par
\f0\par
\b 4. Logging Flow\b0\par
\f1 Container stdout -> Filebeat -> Logstash -> Elasticsearch -> Kibana\par
\f0\par
\b 5. Existing Application Instrumentation\b0\par
The three Node.js services already expose Prometheus endpoints:\par
\f1 http://user-service:4001/metrics\par
http://task-service:4002/metrics\par
http://stats-service:4003/metrics\par
\f0\par
The services use \f1 prom-client\f0 and expose:\par
\f1 http_requests_total\par
process and Node.js default metrics\par
\f0\par
The services use Pino logging. Logs are written as JSON to container stdout, allowing Filebeat to collect them from Kubernetes container log files.\par
\par
