\b 18. Troubleshooting\b0\par
\f1 kubectl get events -n taskflow --sort-by=.lastTimestamp\par
kubectl describe pod <pod-name> -n taskflow\par
\f0\par
\tab \f1 ImagePullBackOff\f0 : pull the image manually with Docker and retry the Pod.\par
\tab \f1 OOMKilled\f0 : reduce JVM heap or increase Docker Desktop memory.\par
\tab \f1 No log index\f0 : check Filebeat connection to Logstash and Logstash connection to Elasticsearch.\par
\tab Kibana has no \f1 wget\f0 in its image; use its readiness status or port-forward instead.\par
\par
\b 19. Cleanup\b0\par
Stop observability components while preserving manifests:\par
\f1 kubectl scale deployment prometheus grafana elasticsearch logstash kibana --replicas=0 -n taskflow\par
kubectl scale daemonset filebeat --replicas=0 -n taskflow\par
\f0\par
Delete observability resources:\par
\f1 kubectl delete -f k8s/logging/04-filebeat.yaml\par
kubectl delete -f k8s/logging/03-elk.yaml\par
kubectl delete -f k8s/monitoring/02-grafana.yaml\par
kubectl delete -f k8s/monitoring/01-prometheus.yaml\par
\f0\par
\b 20. Conclusion\b0\par
Prometheus collected TaskFlow metrics, Grafana provided visual monitoring, Filebeat collected Kubernetes container logs, Logstash processed the logs, Elasticsearch indexed them, and Kibana provided log search and analysis. The implementation demonstrated complete monitoring and centralized logging for a cloud-native microservice application.\par
